WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.360 --> 00:00:16.466
"Are you about your field trip?"

2
00:00:16.827 --> 00:00:19.528
Grandpa asked Laurie as they waited for the school bus.

3
00:00:20.648 --> 00:00:21.449
Laurie shrugged.

4
00:00:21.469 --> 00:00:23.149
"I guess so.

5
00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:29.052
We're just visiting George Washington's headquarters in New Jersey during the American Revolution.

6
00:00:30.208 --> 00:00:33.991
Then we're riding horses through some woods where Washington's soldiers camped."

7
00:00:35.112 --> 00:00:36.173
"That sounds great!"

8
00:00:36.553 --> 00:00:37.314
Grandpa said.

9
00:00:37.334 --> 00:00:41.738
"The American Revolution is my favorite part of American history.

10
00:00:42.819 --> 00:00:48.924
It was an exciting time when colonists were figuring out who they were and what they wanted from

11
00:00:48.964 --> 00:00:49.544
their government.

12
00:00:51.206 --> 00:00:54.108
When it was all over, they had founded a new country."

13
00:00:56.770 --> 00:00:58.312
Laurie couldn't help smiling.

14
00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:03.061
As usual Grandpa was enthusiastic about something in history!

15
00:01:03.081 --> 00:01:09.043
"I know the Revolution had something to do with tea and taxes," she said to him.

16
00:01:09.063 --> 00:01:13.024
"But what were the colonists really fighting about?"

17
00:01:13.044 --> 00:01:18.005
"Independence and the right to decide how they were governed.

18
00:01:19.205 --> 00:01:24.147
Remember, what we know now as American states were British colonies back then.

19
00:01:25.247 --> 00:01:28.708
The founders and original colonists had come from England.

20
00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:37.664
So everyone, even people born in America, were British citizens governed by the British king—King George III.

21
00:01:38.805 --> 00:01:40.066
But he lived in England.

22
00:01:41.346 --> 00:01:47.350
Many colonists started grumbling that he couldn't possibly know what was best for the colonies when he was

23
00:01:47.430 --> 00:01:48.330
so far away.

24
00:01:49.571 --> 00:01:54.714
The really loud grumbling started when King George created the Stamp Act in 1765.

25
00:01:54.774 --> 00:02:02.703
This act said that any paper good sold in the colonies had to have a stamp on it,

26
00:02:03.403 --> 00:02:06.265
and the stamp had to be purchased from a British official.

27
00:02:07.666 --> 00:02:10.028
It was basically a tax paid to England.

28
00:02:11.249 --> 00:02:13.070
So the colonists protested this.

29
00:02:13.090 --> 00:02:17.773
'No taxation without representation,' they said.

30
00:02:18.894 --> 00:02:22.997
That meant: you can't tax us unless we agree on the law together.

31
00:02:24.097 --> 00:02:29.221
Then England expanded the tax to include glass, tea, and other things.

32
00:02:30.522 --> 00:02:34.385
So the colonists decided to boycott British goods.

33
00:02:35.906 --> 00:02:41.090
England didn't like this, and sent soldiers to the colonies to keep things in order.

34
00:02:42.110 --> 00:02:45.993
The war for independence officially broke out in 1775.

35
00:02:46.454 --> 00:02:54.900
The next year the founding fathers of the United States wrote the Declaration of Independence.

36
00:02:55.916 --> 00:03:03.839
This document explained everything that the Americans were fighting for: life, liberty, and the pursuit of happiness.

37
00:03:04.919 --> 00:03:08.440
They wanted to be completely free from British rule."

38
00:03:09.581 --> 00:03:11.782
The school bus rumbled up the street.

39
00:03:12.882 --> 00:03:17.143
"I'm sure you'll learn everything that happened after that on your field trip!

40
00:03:18.104 --> 00:03:18.704
Have fun!"

41
00:03:26.792 --> 00:03:30.053
At four o'clock, Grandpa heard feet pounding on the steps.

42
00:03:32.655 --> 00:03:33.335
"How was it?"

43
00:03:33.675 --> 00:03:36.676
Grandpa asked as his granddaughter bounced into the room.

44
00:03:36.696 --> 00:03:39.217
"So much fun!"

45
00:03:39.638 --> 00:03:40.458
Laurie answered.

46
00:03:40.478 --> 00:03:42.339
"I got you something."

47
00:03:43.459 --> 00:03:45.400
She held out a brown paper bag.

48
00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:51.863
"Thanks!" a quill pen and some ink."

49
00:03:52.923 --> 00:03:54.524
Laurie showed him how to use it.

50
00:03:55.608 --> 00:03:59.690
"First, cut the tip of the quill with a knife to make a slanted edge.

51
00:04:02.632 --> 00:04:05.113
Then, dip that end in the ink bottle.

52
00:04:06.114 --> 00:04:07.715
That's how the colonists wrote.

53
00:04:08.395 --> 00:04:09.115
Cool, huh?

54
00:04:10.036 --> 00:04:12.737
The park ranger who gave us our tour taught me that."

55
00:04:13.918 --> 00:04:15.899
"I bet you learned a lot today.

56
00:04:16.839 --> 00:04:23.203
Now you probably know more than I do about George Washington and the American Revolution," Grandpa said, grinning.

57
00:04:23.223 --> 00:04:26.416
"No!" Laurie protested.

58
00:04:26.436 --> 00:04:31.799
"But I did learn that George Washington was a plantation owner from Virginia.

59
00:04:32.819 --> 00:04:38.501
When the colonists gathered to fight the British, they started an army called the Continental Army.

60
00:04:40.182 --> 00:04:42.243
George Washington became the commander.

61
00:04:43.503 --> 00:04:50.266
He led the soldiers through almost ten years of fighting, moving from colony to colony, until all the

62
00:04:50.326 --> 00:04:51.887
important areas were won.

63
00:04:53.007 --> 00:04:59.054
When the war ended, the colonists, who were now Americans, asked him to be their first president."

64
00:04:59.074 --> 00:05:03.196
"I couldn't have said it better," Grandpa said proudly.

65
00:05:03.216 --> 00:05:07.258
"Now tell me something else you learned today," he said.

66
00:05:07.278 --> 00:05:10.399
"Something that really interested you."

67
00:05:11.540 --> 00:05:12.660
Laurie thought a moment.

68
00:05:12.680 --> 00:05:15.461
"The story of Tempe Wick."

69
00:05:15.481 --> 00:05:17.422
"Tempe Wick?

70
00:05:18.563 --> 00:05:20.824
That sounds like a story I don't know."

71
00:05:22.004 --> 00:05:27.889
"Tempe Wick was a colonial girl who lived on her family's farm, called Wick Farm, in New Jersey.

72
00:05:29.371 --> 00:05:32.994
Tempe loved horses and wasn't afraid of anything.

73
00:05:34.155 --> 00:05:37.758
She was also a proud patriot who believed in the Revolution.

74
00:05:38.819 --> 00:05:44.184
During the war, Washington sent thirteen thousand soldiers to the woods around Wick Farm.

75
00:05:45.205 --> 00:05:48.208
They cleared the land and built log cabins to live in.

76
00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:52.402
But winter in New Jersey was cold and snowy.

77
00:05:53.463 --> 00:05:56.926
The soldiers were freezing, hungry, and tired.

78
00:05:58.287 --> 00:06:00.308
The war seemed like it would never end.

79
00:06:01.109 --> 00:06:02.930
Sometimes the soldiers were bad.

80
00:06:03.951 --> 00:06:09.215
They stole food from nearby farms, and demanded that people give their best horses to them.

81
00:06:10.475 --> 00:06:16.480
One day Tempe was out riding her favorite horse, Colonel, when some Continental soldiers stopped her.

82
00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:21.602
They had just rebelled against their general and were abandoning their camp.

83
00:06:22.762 --> 00:06:25.304
They told her that Colonel was a beautiful horse.

84
00:06:26.424 --> 00:06:29.486
Then they grabbed his reins and said they wanted him!

85
00:06:30.586 --> 00:06:32.747
But Tempe didn't give in to their bullying.

86
00:06:33.868 --> 00:06:35.568
She didn't even get off her horse.

87
00:06:36.829 --> 00:06:39.810
As soon as they let go of the reins, she galloped away!

88
00:06:40.871 --> 00:06:42.892
Tempe knew the soldiers were following her.

89
00:06:44.132 --> 00:06:47.373
She was sure they'd come to the farm and take Colonel away.

90
00:06:49.053 --> 00:06:51.634
So she made a plan to fool the soldiers.

91
00:06:53.155 --> 00:06:57.756
Instead of putting Colonel in the stable, she led him into her bedroom!

92
00:06:58.976 --> 00:07:03.038
When the soldiers came to search the farm, they never thought to look in the house.

93
00:07:04.178 --> 00:07:09.439
The soldiers left empty-handed, and Tempe Wick got to keep her dear Colonel."

94
00:07:10.460 --> 00:07:11.400
Laurie smiled.

95
00:07:12.520 --> 00:07:15.601
"And I got to see her beautiful red cape!

96
00:07:16.722 --> 00:07:19.123
The very same cape she wore that winter.

97
00:07:20.304 --> 00:07:24.405
I can just imagine her riding through the woods like Little Red Riding Hood."

98
00:07:25.906 --> 00:07:27.967
Grandpa laughed loudly and clapped.

99
00:07:30.908 --> 00:07:34.170
"That's a great story!"

100
00:07:34.590 --> 00:07:35.070
he said.

101
00:07:35.090 --> 00:07:38.952
"I like it because it's about a young girl like you.

102
00:07:40.188 --> 00:07:45.830
She was proud to support the Continental soldiers, but she was also brave enough to stand up to

103
00:07:45.870 --> 00:07:47.250
them when they did something wrong."

104
00:07:47.270 --> 00:07:52.372
"Kind of like how the colonists stood up to the British," Laurie said.

105
00:07:52.392 --> 00:07:57.933
"They were courageous, proud of their colonies, and fought to protect their rights."

106
00:07:59.274 --> 00:08:01.234
"Exactly," Grandpa agreed.

107
00:08:01.254 --> 00:08:04.655
"You told that story very well, Laurie."

108
00:08:05.015 --> 00:08:06.596
"Thanks, Grandpa."

109
00:08:08.168 --> 00:08:11.655
"No, thank you—my little historian."
